    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good high-level summary. Can you go a bit deeper into the specific arguments for each? I’m interested in the logical strength of the legal reasoning used by originalists compared to the arguments used by living constitutionalists when it comes to modern firearms regulation.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalists typically base their arguments on a literal reading of the Second Amendment's text, such as the phrase \"the right of the people to keep and bear Arms,\" which they hold to have been written by a specific group of individuals at a particular time, thus fixed in meaning. Living constitutionalists, on the other hand, argue that the meaning of the Second Amendment has evolved with society, and that the language must be interpreted in the context of contemporary concerns and values. Critics of originalism, like Justice Breyer, argue that this approach incorrectly treats the Constitution as a historical artifact rather than a living document capable of responding to changing societal needs.",
      "source": "target",
