    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see. One thing that strikes me is the level of detail in the originalist argument—it seems to rely on a very structured, textual framework. When looking at the living constitutionalist side, though, it feels a lot more subjective, like it's just based on whatever the current social mood happens to be. Does legal scholarship generally view the originalist method as being more grounded in objective legal theory than the living constitutionalist approach?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Many legal scholars, including some originalists themselves, acknowledge that the originalist method can be subject to criticisms ofistorical revisionism, textual interpretation biases, and the inherent difficulties in reconstructing historical intentions. Some originalists, like Justice Thomas, have acknowledged these limitations and attempted to ground their interpretation in more persuasive linguistic and historical arguments, but the originalist method still often relies on textual fiat. In contrast, living constitutionalists might be accused of inconsistent or intractable application of principles, although proponents like Justice Breyer argue that the Constitution's meaning evolves to address changing societal values and technological advancements, which allows for a more adaptive and reasonable reading of the Constitution.",
      "source": "target",
